2
00:00:03,338 --> 00:00:06,222
<i>What is
the perfect American family?</i>

3
00:00:06,256 --> 00:00:09,274
<i>Who knows exactly? I do know,
out here in the middle,</i>

4
00:00:09,308 --> 00:00:11,442
<i>it's something
we all strive for.</i>

5
00:00:11,476 --> 00:00:14,444
Get your heads out of your butts
and move, people!

6
00:00:14,478 --> 00:00:16,478
We are not gonna be late
for church again!

7
00:00:16,513 --> 00:00:19,781
<i>Yeah, we're still striving.
I am sick of this, Axl!</i>

8
00:00:19,815 --> 00:00:23,116
- You are always moving the arrow to my name.
- It's your turn!

9
00:00:23,151 --> 00:00:26,118
- It was my turn to load the dishwasher yesterday.
- Yeah, but mom brought home pasta bread bowls,

10
00:00:26,153 --> 00:00:29,553
<i>so actually we ate the dishes,
so that makes it your turn, so...</i>

11
00:00:29,571 --> 00:00:32,555
<i- >Ha!
But it's not my fault that mom's too lazy to cook,</i>

12
00:00:32,573 --> 00:00:34,556
so it's not my problem,
it's your problem.

13
00:00:34,590 --> 00:00:37,324
Ohh! Swoosh! Nothin' but net.
Game over. Ooh!

14
00:00:37,359 --> 00:00:39,826
Ugh! Would you two stop it?
That's the whole reason

15
00:00:39,860 --> 00:00:42,661
I made the stupid chore chart
is to avoid all this.

16
00:00:42,695 --> 00:00:46,364
Tell him! It's his turn! Look, I am sorry.
I don't know whose turn it is to do what,

17
00:00:46,398 --> 00:00:48,865
and we're in a hurry,
so the arrow decides.

18
00:00:48,900 --> 00:00:50,732
Sweet.

19
00:00:50,750 --> 00:00:52,734
Not fair.
Spin again.

20
00:00:52,752 --> 00:00:55,703
The arrow has spoken. I am not kidding!
You heard your mother! Why am I yelling?

21
00:00:55,737 --> 00:00:57,704
Dishwasher. What's the matter with you?
You're both

22
00:00:57,738 --> 00:01:00,573
practically adults now. It's
time to start acting like it.

23
00:01:00,591 --> 00:01:03,341
That the general idea of what
you were going for?
Yeah.

24
00:01:03,376 --> 00:01:05,843
Oh, great.
Now brick's in the front seat.

25
00:01:05,877 --> 00:01:08,344
He's probably seconds away from
putting the car in gear, so go.

26
00:01:08,379 --> 00:01:10,179
Go!
Ugh!

27
00:01:10,213 --> 00:01:12,797
Did you
remember to take the car in?

28
00:01:12,848 --> 00:01:14,848
That warning
light's on again.

29
00:01:14,882 --> 00:01:16,817
Yes, I remembered,
but I didn't do it,

30
00:01:16,851 --> 00:01:18,818
'cause I called
the exterminator,

31
00:01:18,852 --> 00:01:21,586
and it's your turn.
Nope. It's your turn,
'cause I fixed the garbage disposal,

32
00:01:21,604 --> 00:01:24,304
- and I'm buried at work right now.
- Well, where do you think I go every day?

33
00:01:24,355 --> 00:01:26,856
<i>I'm not coming home beaten down
and dejected from the spa.</i>

34
00:01:26,890 --> 00:01:30,592
And if you're looking to help
others, and God hopes you are,

35
00:01:30,626 --> 00:01:34,027
we still need host families to
place foreign exchange students.

36
00:01:34,061 --> 00:01:37,563
When you load the dishwasher,
I didn't rinse any of my plates,

37
00:01:37,647 --> 00:01:39,564
so you got some
really nasty scraping

38
00:01:39,598 --> 00:01:43,099
<i>ahead of you. Normally Sue
would let things slide and just take it,</i>

39
00:01:43,134 --> 00:01:45,568
<i>but it was her time
of the month...</i>

40
00:01:45,602 --> 00:01:49,137
<i>Aah! The time when she gets
her braces tightened.</i>

41
00:01:49,171 --> 00:01:50,804
Aah!

42
00:01:50,839 --> 00:01:54,140
Mike.
Love your sister.

43
00:01:54,174 --> 00:01:57,342
Now bless you all,
and we hope to see you next Sunday.

44
00:02:01,178 --> 00:02:05,113
So, Frankie, are you guys gonna
host a foreign exchange student?

45
00:02:05,148 --> 00:02:07,982
We did it last year,
and we're doing it again.

46
00:02:08,016 --> 00:02:09,950
You get so much from it.

47
00:02:09,984 --> 00:02:12,818
Oh, wow. Gosh, Nancy, we would
love to, but we're so busy,

48
00:02:12,853 --> 00:02:15,186
what with... recycling
and everything.

49
00:02:15,221 --> 00:02:17,571
Believe me, it's
the most rewarding experience

50
00:02:17,605 --> 00:02:19,889
for the whole family,
especially the kids.

51
00:02:19,907 --> 00:02:23,091
Really?
Oh, it totally changes them.

52
00:02:23,125 --> 00:02:25,526
Being immersed with someone
from a different culture

53
00:02:25,544 --> 00:02:27,460
gives them
this global experience

54
00:02:27,495 --> 00:02:29,895
and makes them
more tolerant and mature.

55
00:02:29,929 --> 00:02:32,630
Mom,
Axl wiped a booger on me!

56
00:02:37,385 --> 00:02:39,201
Listen, guys...

57
00:02:39,236 --> 00:02:42,220
That was the reverend,
and he says there's a bunch

58
00:02:42,254 --> 00:02:45,372
of foreign exchange students still available.
I'm sure there are.

59
00:02:45,406 --> 00:02:48,107
Remember at church today?

60
00:02:48,141 --> 00:02:50,542
- I was talking to Nancy Donahue, and she...
- Oh, man.

61
00:02:50,576 --> 00:02:53,911
And she said it's
a very rewarding experience

62
00:02:53,945 --> 00:02:56,012
that makes kids more tolerant.

63
00:02:56,046 --> 00:02:58,413
And our kids could use some more
tolerance, Mike, and guess what?

64
00:02:58,448 --> 00:03:01,315
There's a kid in Japan who would
love to come to Indiana. Why?

65
00:03:01,350 --> 00:03:03,250
A foreign exchange kid?
I don't know, Frankie.

66
00:03:03,284 --> 00:03:05,718
We have enough trouble paying
attention to the kids we have.

67
00:03:05,752 --> 00:03:08,453
Do we get to exchange someone?
'Cause I vote for Axl.

68
00:03:08,487 --> 00:03:11,321
I'm in. This is way better than
the dog you wouldn't let me get.

69
00:03:11,356 --> 00:03:13,256
Can we name him "Rex"?

70
00:03:13,290 --> 00:03:15,324
Guys, guys,
it's not about that.

71
00:03:15,359 --> 00:03:17,726
This is about us hosting
someone, making a difference.

72
00:03:17,760 --> 00:03:21,361
We'd be like a pebble
that makes a tiny ripple

73
00:03:21,395 --> 00:03:24,730
that becomes this wave...
A wave that starts in Orson

74
00:03:24,764 --> 00:03:28,299
and becomes bigger and bigger
all the way to Japan.

75
00:03:28,333 --> 00:03:30,333
Those big waves are called
"tsunamis"...

76
00:03:30,351 --> 00:03:33,252
And they don't like them.

77
00:03:33,286 --> 00:03:35,470
Mike, I'm serious. We have
a real opportunity here.

78
00:03:35,504 --> 00:03:38,372
We'll be like ambassadors.
Then he goes out in the world

79
00:03:38,406 --> 00:03:40,373
and tells people
how great it is here.

80
00:03:40,407 --> 00:03:42,941
And pretty soon,
America's popular again.

81
00:03:42,975 --> 00:03:44,776
Come on, Mike.

82
00:03:44,810 --> 00:03:48,177
I talked you into hummus,
and you ended up liking that.

83
00:03:48,212 --> 00:03:51,380
I'm down, but as long as it's
some super hot girl from Finland

84
00:03:51,414 --> 00:03:54,966
or Sweden. Is there some sort
of catalog we can look at?

85
00:03:55,017 --> 00:03:57,383
Hey, you get
what they give you,

86
00:03:57,401 --> 00:03:59,818
just like we did with you guys.

87
00:03:59,836 --> 00:04:01,753
<i>The day had finally arrived.
The house was clean.</i>

88
00:04:01,787 --> 00:04:03,254
<i>The kids were clean.</i>

89
00:04:03,289 --> 00:04:06,223
<i>I even got Mike
to power wash all the cobwebs</i>

90
00:04:06,290 --> 00:04:07,723
<i>off the screens.</i>

91
00:04:07,758 --> 00:04:09,724
<i>Yeah, I should've done this
years ago.</i>

92
00:04:09,759 --> 00:04:11,693
God, this is so exciting.

93
00:04:11,727 --> 00:04:13,627
We're actually in
the international section

94
00:04:13,662 --> 00:04:15,128
of the airport.

95
00:04:15,162 --> 00:04:17,063
Those people over there
just got off the plane

96
00:04:17,097 --> 00:04:18,864
<i>from Toronto... Canada.</i>

97
00:04:18,898 --> 00:04:20,831
I'm proud of us, Mike.

98
00:04:20,866 --> 00:04:23,700
We're actually gonna change
someone's life,

99
00:04:23,734 --> 00:04:25,601
and they're gonna change ours.
Who knows?

100
00:04:25,635 --> 00:04:28,536
Maybe next year we'll be spending
Christmas in Japan.

101
00:04:28,571 --> 00:04:31,321
They celebrate that, right?
I hear they got a lot of malls there, so probably.

102
00:04:31,356 --> 00:04:34,173
Yeah. Takayuki?

103
00:04:34,207 --> 00:04:36,675
Hey!
You made it! Hi.

104
00:04:36,742 --> 00:04:39,927
I don't know
if you're a hugger,

105
00:04:39,978 --> 00:04:42,395
but you're gettin' one.
Ohh.

106
00:04:42,429 --> 00:04:44,379
Welcome to America.
Uh, I'm Frankie.

107
00:04:44,414 --> 00:04:45,864
This is Mike...

108
00:04:45,915 --> 00:04:47,882
Axl...

109
00:04:47,916 --> 00:04:51,351
Brick... and Sue.
We are your family.

110
00:04:51,385 --> 00:04:53,719
Okay.

111
00:04:53,753 --> 00:04:55,153
Okay.

112
00:04:55,187 --> 00:04:57,921
Oh! Can Takayuki sit
next to me on the way home?

113
00:04:57,956 --> 00:05:00,424
No, he's mine.
Mom got him for me.

114
00:05:00,458 --> 00:05:03,291
Brick, I got him for everybody.

115
00:05:03,325 --> 00:05:05,794
Oh!
We know you're not a pet.

116
00:05:05,828 --> 00:05:08,128
You probably
noticed my sweatshirt.

117
00:05:08,162 --> 00:05:10,046
I'm on cross-country.

118
00:05:10,097 --> 00:05:12,064
I'm what you call
a "jock."

119
00:05:12,098 --> 00:05:13,732
Itch.

120
00:05:13,766 --> 00:05:15,216
Don't start!

121
00:05:15,267 --> 00:05:17,434
Okay, Takayuki, get ready,

122
00:05:17,468 --> 00:05:20,603
'cause we're gonna show you
America!

123
00:05:20,637 --> 00:05:22,337
Whoo-hoo!

124
00:05:26,468 --> 00:05:28,692
I don't know what you watch
at your house,

125
00:05:28,726 --> 00:05:32,039
but we have all kinds of great shows here...

126
00:05:32,073 --> 00:05:34,030
"The Bachelor," "Bachelorette,"
"Say Yes To The Dress,"

127
00:05:34,064 --> 00:05:36,673
"Ace Of Cakes."
Oh.

128
00:05:36,741 --> 00:05:39,199
Don't think all American girls
are Lindsay Lohan.

129
00:05:39,234 --> 00:05:42,060
I'm taking an aspirin, 'cause I
just got my braces tightened.

130
00:05:42,095 --> 00:05:45,357
They're correcting
an overbite-crossbite.

131
00:05:45,391 --> 00:05:48,803
Do you have
braces in Japan?

132
00:05:48,838 --> 00:05:52,517
Do you read any anime
or manga books? I have lots.

133
00:05:52,551 --> 00:05:53,990
I'd love to get

134
00:05:54,025 --> 00:05:56,834
an actual Japanese person's
insight.

135
00:05:56,869 --> 00:05:59,879
No, I do not read them,

136
00:05:59,913 --> 00:06:02,523
and, yes, we have braces.

137
00:06:02,557 --> 00:06:05,400
Oh, gosh. I'm sorry.
You're probably jet-lagged,

138
00:06:05,435 --> 00:06:08,311
and here we are pestering you
with a bunch of questions.

139
00:06:08,346 --> 00:06:12,093
We're just so
excited to have you here.

140
00:06:12,127 --> 00:06:14,469
Mnh. No!
Those are my fries.

141
00:06:14,503 --> 00:06:17,346
Axl! Why don't you and Sue clean
up and take your empty bags

142
00:06:17,381 --> 00:06:19,454
to the kitchen, please?
Thank you.

143
00:06:21,229 --> 00:06:23,537
Uh, you know, Takayuki,

144
00:06:23,571 --> 00:06:25,946
you can eat what's in that bag.

145
00:06:25,981 --> 00:06:27,519
That's yours.

146
00:06:48,199 --> 00:06:50,038
No, thank you.

147
00:06:50,073 --> 00:06:53,619
Axl! You are so dead!
I'm sick of this, Axl!

148
00:06:53,637 --> 00:06:56,798
That's American teen lingo.
"Dead" means "cool."

149
00:06:56,866 --> 00:07:01,048
I wouldn't use that, though.
Excuse me.

150
00:07:01,082 --> 00:07:03,206
Axl! Ugh!
Stop it!

151
00:07:03,241 --> 00:07:06,133
You want him to think we're
animals? We are ambassadors!

152
00:07:06,151 --> 00:07:08,543
<i>Axl, moved the arrow again!</i>

153
00:07:08,578 --> 00:07:10,484
Sue, get back in there
and entertain our guest!

154
00:07:10,519 --> 00:07:12,877
I will straighten out
your brother.

155
00:07:14,685 --> 00:07:16,708
<i>Axl, I have had it with...</i>

156
00:07:16,743 --> 00:07:18,682
Okay, look, I'll pay you $10

157
00:07:18,717 --> 00:07:20,624
to get along with your sister
while Takayuki's here.

158
00:07:20,658 --> 00:07:22,096
Yeah.

159
00:07:22,130 --> 00:07:24,739
And that's why we have
three branches of government.

160
00:07:26,581 --> 00:07:28,235
Oh, Frankie's back.

161
00:07:28,270 --> 00:07:29,925
So...

162
00:07:29,960 --> 00:07:32,335
Tell us about your family.

163
00:07:32,369 --> 00:07:35,012
Do you have
any brothers or sisters?

164
00:07:35,047 --> 00:07:36,869
Yes.

165
00:07:36,903 --> 00:07:39,195
Yes, you have brothers,
or, yes, you have sisters?

166
00:07:39,229 --> 00:07:40,634
Or yes for both?

167
00:07:40,668 --> 00:07:42,775
Yes.

168
00:07:42,810 --> 00:07:44,950
Okay. Well, good.

169
00:07:44,984 --> 00:07:47,326
Is there anything
you want to know about us?

170
00:07:47,361 --> 00:07:49,902
No.

171
00:07:49,937 --> 00:07:52,780
Well, we could just gab
all night, but, you know,

172
00:07:52,814 --> 00:07:55,056
it's gettin' late,
so, Takayuki,

173
00:07:55,090 --> 00:07:57,499
why don't I show you
to your room?

174
00:07:57,533 --> 00:07:59,707
<i>Alright,
so it was a little rough,</i>

175
00:07:59,741 --> 00:08:02,049
<i>but it was only his first day.</i>

176
00:08:02,084 --> 00:08:05,077
<i>I knew Takayuki just needed
a good night's sleep</i>

177
00:08:05,113 --> 00:08:07,503
<i>and a good old American breakfast.</i>

178
00:08:07,538 --> 00:08:09,010
Guess what?

179
00:08:09,044 --> 00:08:11,686
Today you're going
to school with Axl.

180
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:15,501
Ooh.
Senior hotties. Okay. Here's the plan.

181
00:08:15,519 --> 00:08:18,446
When they walk by,
look confused and ask for help.

182
00:08:18,481 --> 00:08:20,889
They'll stop
'cause you're exotic.

183
00:08:20,923 --> 00:08:23,833
You tell them how great I am.
I show up. You get the ugly one.

184
00:08:23,867 --> 00:08:26,159
Got it?

185
00:08:26,210 --> 00:08:28,518
Oh, my gosh.
Did he really?

186
00:08:30,493 --> 00:08:31,931
Dude.

187
00:08:33,404 --> 00:08:37,118
<i>Worst day of my life,
and I live here!</i>

188
00:08:37,152 --> 00:08:39,594
It's only his second day.

189
00:08:45,953 --> 00:08:48,361
Did you know
that 85% of your people

190
00:08:48,395 --> 00:08:50,469
have never tasted turkey

191
00:08:50,503 --> 00:08:53,731
<i>and that your national bird
is the kiji?</i>

192
00:08:53,766 --> 00:08:55,305
<i>Kiji.</i>

193
00:08:55,356 --> 00:08:57,228
Ours is the bald eagle.

194
00:08:57,262 --> 00:08:59,638
Benjamin Franklin wanted it
to be the turkey,

195
00:08:59,672 --> 00:09:02,214
which 85% of your people
have never tasted.

196
00:09:02,249 --> 00:09:04,188
Isn't that funny?

197
00:09:04,223 --> 00:09:06,130
Yes.

198
00:09:11,736 --> 00:09:15,700
Well, that was exhausting.

199
00:09:15,734 --> 00:09:18,042
It's only his third day.

200
00:09:23,999 --> 00:09:27,377
♪ Don't go breaking my heart ♪

201
00:09:33,118 --> 00:09:35,375
♪ Don't... ♪

202
00:09:35,409 --> 00:09:37,785
♪ nobody know... ♪

203
00:09:40,027 --> 00:09:41,901
I just sang karaoke

204
00:09:41,935 --> 00:09:43,824
<i>for three hours
and nothing!</i>

205
00:09:43,859 --> 00:09:46,167
And Brick told me
it's their national pastime!

206
00:09:47,723 --> 00:09:49,664
It's only his fourth day!

207
00:10:07,064 --> 00:10:09,439
Frankie, I think we got a dud.

208
00:10:09,473 --> 00:10:12,350
Mike, he is not a dud.

209
00:10:12,386 --> 00:10:15,296
I'm with dad.
I tried to play bananagrams with him.

210
00:10:15,363 --> 00:10:18,273
He went to the bathroom
and never came out.

211
00:10:18,308 --> 00:10:19,746
He's like a cat.

212
00:10:19,781 --> 00:10:21,787
You show him affection,
but he doesn't return it.

213
00:10:21,822 --> 00:10:24,397
He's just a shy kid
who's only been here for...

214
00:10:24,432 --> 00:10:27,208
Five days.
That's right. For five days!

215
00:10:27,242 --> 00:10:30,487
You can't expect somebody
to come halfway around the world

216
00:10:30,521 --> 00:10:33,566
and just jump in and become
a part of the family.

217
00:10:33,600 --> 00:10:36,343
It takes time. You call yourselves ambassadors.
No, we didn't. You called us that.

218
00:10:36,378 --> 00:10:38,267
And I'm sorry I did.

219
00:10:38,319 --> 00:10:41,129
Now I am going to the Donahue's'
to borrow their toaster,

220
00:10:41,163 --> 00:10:44,006
because ours is starting
to spark, and while I'm there,

221
00:10:44,041 --> 00:10:46,348
I want you to think about
improving your attitude.

222
00:10:46,383 --> 00:10:49,025
I know Takayuki doesn't say
how disappointed he is,

223
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:52,572
but his vacant stare
speaks volumes.

224
00:10:54,447 --> 00:10:57,390
Mom. Mom. I
threw Axl's pillow on the roof,

225
00:10:57,408 --> 00:10:59,665
and now he says he's gonna
get revenge, and...

226
00:10:59,684 --> 00:11:02,578
Sue, I don't want to hear it.
I'm laying down the law!

227
00:11:02,612 --> 00:11:05,890
Here's a $5.
Love your brother.

228
00:11:11,714 --> 00:11:14,423
Hi, Takayuki.
How was fishing?

229
00:11:14,458 --> 00:11:15,929
Okay.

230
00:11:15,963 --> 00:11:19,509
Oh, you didn't like
your French toast dippers?

231
00:11:21,518 --> 00:11:23,893
Takayuki, I'm kind of
at a loss here.

232
00:11:23,928 --> 00:11:26,218
I've been making you
breakfast every morning,

233
00:11:26,253 --> 00:11:28,143
and you're not eating it.

234
00:11:28,177 --> 00:11:30,937
How do you know you don't like
it, if you don't even try it?

235
00:11:30,971 --> 00:11:32,844
I mean, these are really good.

236
00:11:32,878 --> 00:11:36,207
They have maple flavoring
baked right in. Mmm. See?

237
00:11:40,491 --> 00:11:41,895
You know what?

238
00:11:41,930 --> 00:11:43,519
I'll be back.

239
00:11:45,945 --> 00:11:48,538
Hey, Nancy. Let me help.

240
00:11:48,572 --> 00:11:51,014
Oh, thanks, Frankie.
It's my third trip

241
00:11:51,048 --> 00:11:54,360
- to the grocery store this week.
- Oh, you, too? I mean,

242
00:11:54,395 --> 00:11:56,953
isn't it hard to find something
your exchange student will eat?

243
00:11:56,988 --> 00:11:59,496
Oh, no. Ours is
practically a chef.

244
00:12:01,672 --> 00:12:03,546
From another region of Spain.

245
00:12:03,580 --> 00:12:05,587
<I>Last night was tapas from...
Bar-thelona.

246
00:12:05,621 --> 00:12:07,494
Oh, yeah.
Oh, us, too.

247
00:12:07,528 --> 00:12:10,806
Boy, we are having a blast
over there. It's so much fun.

248
00:12:10,841 --> 00:12:12,212
Oh, isn't it?

249
00:12:12,247 --> 00:12:14,053
Esteban plays guitar

250
00:12:14,087 --> 00:12:16,261
and has been teaching us
flamenco dancing.

251
00:12:16,295 --> 00:12:19,155
Ooh. He's such a treasure. I'm
sure you all feel the same way.

252
00:12:19,206 --> 00:12:22,987
Oh, yeah.
Feelings. So many feelings.

253
00:12:23,021 --> 00:12:25,496
You should get together
with the Taylors,

254
00:12:25,531 --> 00:12:28,006
'cause they got assigned
a boy from Japan, too,

255
00:12:28,041 --> 00:12:30,465
and they're having a blast.

256
00:12:30,501 --> 00:12:32,306
<i>Mama, I made rebujitos.</i>

257
00:12:32,341 --> 00:12:34,699
<i>Gra-thias, Esteban.
Oh.</i>

258
00:12:34,733 --> 00:12:36,807
Come.

259
00:12:36,842 --> 00:12:39,785
<i>I brought a game from Spain
called "patim patam patum."</i>

260
00:12:39,819 --> 00:12:42,495
There is lots of running
and pushing,

261
00:12:42,513 --> 00:12:45,440
with many laughs between players.
Let's play! Ooh.

262
00:12:53,087 --> 00:12:55,579
We got a dud.

263
00:12:56,563 --> 00:12:58,476
You guys were right. Takayuki's
a dud. We got ripped off.

264
00:12:58,510 --> 00:13:00,204
Duh.
Told you.

265
00:13:00,238 --> 00:13:02,705
That's what we've been telling you.
He's no hummus.

266
00:13:02,739 --> 00:13:05,106
What are you drinking?
Nancy's Esteban made it.

267
00:13:05,140 --> 00:13:07,539
He's been cooking, dancing,
teaching them games.

268
00:13:07,574 --> 00:13:09,554
They're getting
this amazing global experience,

269
00:13:09,588 --> 00:13:12,003
and we're hiding from ours
in the bedroom.

270
00:13:12,039 --> 00:13:15,193
Dog's looking better and better, isn't it?
I wanted a cultural exchange,

271
00:13:15,228 --> 00:13:17,845
and instead I get
another sullen teen.

272
00:13:17,879 --> 00:13:20,765
I mean, what's with his parents,
sending him over here?

273
00:13:20,800 --> 00:13:24,391
I would never saddle some
poor Japanese family with Axl.

274
00:13:24,425 --> 00:13:27,278
Hey, I would
totally rage in Japan.

275
00:13:27,313 --> 00:13:30,065
You know, I'm tempted to write
the agency a letter. You won't.

276
00:13:30,099 --> 00:13:32,145
Yeah, probably not,
but I should.

277
00:13:32,180 --> 00:13:34,697
Well, we're just
gonna have to get through

278
00:13:34,732 --> 00:13:37,181
one more week with him.

279
00:13:37,216 --> 00:13:39,044
I know. I know.

280
00:13:39,079 --> 00:13:41,981
Now what? Wait. We can't
all leave at the same time.

281
00:13:41,999 --> 00:13:44,499
He'll think we were in here
talking about him.

282
00:13:44,534 --> 00:13:47,419
Wait a few minutes,
then come out one at a time.

283
00:13:47,438 --> 00:13:50,373
But not the same amount of time,
or it'll look like a pattern.

284
00:13:50,408 --> 00:13:52,287
Stagger it.

285
00:13:52,305 --> 00:13:56,483
But vary it, so the stagger
doesn't look like a pattern.

286
00:13:58,062 --> 00:14:00,361
Oh, sorry, Frankie,
but I forgot to ask,

287
00:14:00,412 --> 00:14:02,526
could you take in our mail
this week?

288
00:14:02,561 --> 00:14:05,077
We're taking Esteban
to cedar point.

289
00:14:05,111 --> 00:14:07,545
Wow. That's quite a trip.

290
00:14:07,546 --> 00:14:11,489
Well, we already hit all
the hot spots in Orson... the zoo,

291
00:14:11,523 --> 00:14:13,805
the Amish general store,
pioneer galaxy mall.

292
00:14:13,840 --> 00:14:17,263
He went berserk for kettle corn,
and we just take it for granted.

293
00:14:17,281 --> 00:14:20,050
Well, I never will again.

294
00:14:20,084 --> 00:14:22,633
So, um, y-you've been
taking him places?

295
00:14:22,652 --> 00:14:25,723
Oh, yes. It's an important part
of the cultural immersion.

296
00:14:25,757 --> 00:14:28,441
Can I tell you,
when we first t Esteban,

297
00:14:28,477 --> 00:14:30,624
he was so shy
he hardly said a word?

298
00:14:30,658 --> 00:14:32,135
Esteban?

299
00:14:32,169 --> 00:14:33,745
<i>"Pitum patum putum" Esteban?</i>

300
00:14:33,781 --> 00:14:36,196
Uh-huh. And then we started
taking him places,

301
00:14:36,214 --> 00:14:39,016
and he opened right up.

302
00:14:39,051 --> 00:14:41,164
I mean, they didn't fly
halfway around the world

303
00:14:41,200 --> 00:14:43,615
just to sit
in front of a TV, right?

304
00:14:43,649 --> 00:14:46,804
No, they did not.

305
00:14:46,839 --> 00:14:49,994
<i>We're the duds.</i>

306
00:14:50,028 --> 00:14:52,914
What? That's right.
It's not him.

307
00:14:52,949 --> 00:14:55,264
It's us.
We're a horrible host family.

308
00:14:55,299 --> 00:14:57,245
Oh, no. Hang on.

309
00:14:57,279 --> 00:15:00,702
When you left, we were all in agreement
that he's the dud. Yeah, well, he's not.

310
00:15:00,737 --> 00:15:03,689
I was talking to Nancy Donahue
and she... oh.

311
00:15:03,725 --> 00:15:06,946
I swear, I'm building a fence
in front of that place!

312
00:15:06,980 --> 00:15:08,910
It's total immersion
over there, Mike.

313
00:15:08,961 --> 00:15:11,411
They have toured Esteban
everywhere, and all we've done

314
00:15:11,445 --> 00:15:14,365
is work, eat dinner, and sit
and watch "The Bachelor."

315
00:15:14,399 --> 00:15:16,329
No wonder he's so sullen.

316
00:15:16,380 --> 00:15:19,064
We haven't done anything fun
with him.

317
00:15:19,100 --> 00:15:22,389
In our defense, we don't do
anything fun with our own kids.

318
00:15:22,423 --> 00:15:25,712
You know what we are? We're lazy.
Lazy, lazy ambassadors.

319
00:15:28,197 --> 00:15:30,612
God, what was I thinking?
Breakfast from the toaster.

320
00:15:30,647 --> 00:15:34,205
<i>No wonder he just stared at me.
That's what you give us.</i>

321
00:15:34,239 --> 00:15:36,185
Yeah, but you're
from here already.

322
00:15:36,220 --> 00:15:39,911
Your body's used to junk.
Actually rice is the main staple
of the Japanese breakfast.

323
00:15:39,946 --> 00:15:42,194
How long you been sittin'
on that one, Brick?

324
00:15:42,228 --> 00:15:44,578
Look, we gotta
put in some effort,

325
00:15:44,596 --> 00:15:46,575
show him a fun time.

326
00:15:46,626 --> 00:15:49,344
We need Americana...

327
00:15:49,363 --> 00:15:51,527
Fall foliage, candle making,

328
00:15:51,561 --> 00:15:53,004
old-timey train rides,

329
00:15:53,038 --> 00:15:55,118
meat on sticks!

330
00:15:55,153 --> 00:15:57,569
We're taking him
to Brown County!

331
00:16:02,337 --> 00:16:05,256
So, Takayuki, you're
gonna love brown county.

332
00:16:05,274 --> 00:16:06,767
Look at this.

333
00:16:06,802 --> 00:16:09,218
"Visit our replica
of a real pioneer village

334
00:16:09,252 --> 00:16:11,769
"from days of yore,
where we churn butter

335
00:16:11,803 --> 00:16:13,750
"and sew cozies for just about

336
00:16:13,784 --> 00:16:16,200
any small major appliance
you can think of."

337
00:16:16,235 --> 00:16:18,550
Wow, huh?

338
00:16:18,584 --> 00:16:20,632
Yes. Wow.

339
00:16:20,666 --> 00:16:23,115
Dad, is there
a rest stop coming up?

340
00:16:23,133 --> 00:16:26,539
<i>I have to go,
if you know what I mean.</i>

341
00:16:26,575 --> 00:16:28,520
Yeah, I think
I can crack that code.

342
00:16:28,554 --> 00:16:30,500
There's a sports bar
in about 10 miles.

343
00:16:30,535 --> 00:16:32,481
You can do your business,
and I can catch

344
00:16:32,515 --> 00:16:35,166
some of the colts game.
It's a win-win.

345
00:16:37,316 --> 00:16:39,229
This is great, Takayuki.

346
00:16:39,263 --> 00:16:41,746
We're having
some good old American fun...

347
00:16:41,780 --> 00:16:45,104
Some fun you can tell
your whole country about.

348
00:16:53,799 --> 00:16:57,558
Classic. You're lucky
you get to leave in a week.

349
00:16:57,592 --> 00:16:59,505
This is my life.

350
00:17:02,671 --> 00:17:04,595
This is just a temporary setback.

351
00:17:04,699 --> 00:17:06,548
See, we've got warning lights
going off in our car

352
00:17:06,658 --> 00:17:09,298
all the time. Usually it doesn't
mean anything, but...

353
00:17:09,409 --> 00:17:13,055
I guess this time it did.
It was Mike's turn to check.

354
00:17:13,543 --> 00:17:15,995
<i>Mom, I really have to go.</i>

355
00:17:16,046 --> 00:17:18,982
The tow truck guy will
be here in a couple hours.

356
00:17:19,016 --> 00:17:21,801
Says the game's too exciting
to leave now.

357
00:17:21,836 --> 00:17:25,623
No! Oh, my God.
It's getting really bad.

358
00:17:25,641 --> 00:17:27,625
Boo-yah!
That's what you get

359
00:17:27,643 --> 00:17:30,729
- for messing with the Ax-man's pillow. Mm!
- What?

360
00:17:30,763 --> 00:17:32,180
Yeah. Yeah.

361
00:17:32,232 --> 00:17:35,184
Remember that aspirin I gave you
earlier when your braces hurt?

362
00:17:35,236 --> 00:17:37,237
It was one of mom's speedy-lax.

363
00:17:37,271 --> 00:17:38,688
Boo-yah! Uhh!

364
00:17:38,739 --> 00:17:42,543
Are you crazy?!
What... I... Mom, help!

365
00:17:42,577 --> 00:17:45,497
Axl! Sorry, honey.
I-I don't know what to tell ya.

366
00:17:45,531 --> 00:17:48,783
If it's really that bad,
you'll have to use the ditch.

367
00:17:48,818 --> 00:17:50,953
What?! I can't go
in a...

368
00:17:50,987 --> 00:17:53,989
Axl!
You are in big trouble!

369
00:17:54,007 --> 00:17:57,027
Told you
to get along with your sister,

370
00:17:57,061 --> 00:17:59,062
and... You know what?
I want my $20 back. Now!

371
00:17:59,097 --> 00:18:02,000
No way! The $20 was
to come on the trip.

372
00:18:02,034 --> 00:18:05,003
The $10 earlier...
That was to be nice to Sue.

373
00:18:05,037 --> 00:18:07,606
What? I only got $5!
No fair! It's not... ooh.

374
00:18:09,842 --> 00:18:12,228
- What's this about?
- Mom's paying us.

375
00:18:12,280 --> 00:18:14,180
- She is?
- Frankie.

376
00:18:14,215 --> 00:18:17,685
- Okay, look...
- You're seriously paying our kids
to do things

377
00:18:17,736 --> 00:18:21,289
- they should be doing in the first place?
- Not everybody.

378
00:18:21,323 --> 00:18:22,924
Let me explain.

379
00:18:22,959 --> 00:18:26,628
I'll give you
30 bucks not to have this fight

380
00:18:26,663 --> 00:18:29,716
right now in front of Takayuki.
What is with you?
You've been nuts ever since he got here.

381
00:18:29,750 --> 00:18:32,335
I knew this was a mistake,

382
00:18:32,369 --> 00:18:34,938
- but, no, you just had to have this kid.
- Are you talking about me?

383
00:18:34,973 --> 00:18:38,226
No. Once we knew you were coming, we were happy about it.
I just wanted to impress Takayuki

384
00:18:38,260 --> 00:18:40,412
by showing him
what a great family we are!

385
00:18:40,446 --> 00:18:42,880
Axl, I am going to kill you!

386
00:18:42,898 --> 00:18:45,935
He took a picture of me going!
It's just her head...

387
00:18:45,986 --> 00:18:48,387
Ooh! But when I explain
it on Facebook,

388
00:18:48,405 --> 00:18:50,624
- everybody's gonna get it.
- Give me that phone!

389
00:18:50,658 --> 00:18:53,527
No!
- Seriously. Let it go!
- Here. I...

390
00:18:53,561 --> 00:18:56,331
- Hey! Don't you touch my cell phone!
- Give it to me!

391
00:18:56,365 --> 00:18:59,968
- Give me the phone. Give me the phone!
- We are ambassadors!
- Ambassadors don't sit in the way back.

392
00:19:00,003 --> 00:19:02,471
Why don't you just
pay 'em, Frankie?

393
00:19:05,409 --> 00:19:06,876
I got money!

394
00:19:06,910 --> 00:19:09,912
Who likes money?

395
00:19:13,802 --> 00:19:15,887
Well, congratulations,
Takayuki!

396
00:19:15,922 --> 00:19:18,457
You wanted to see America?

397
00:19:18,491 --> 00:19:21,994
You're seeing it.
This is an American family.

398
00:19:22,029 --> 00:19:24,447
Yeah, we yell and fight,
we eat bad food,

399
00:19:24,481 --> 00:19:28,335
- we watch too much TV, and we bribe our kids.
- Not all of them.

400
00:19:28,370 --> 00:19:30,304
Give it a rest, Brick.

401
00:19:30,338 --> 00:19:32,741
Maybe you're horrified
by all of this.

402
00:19:32,775 --> 00:19:35,877
Or maybe you're not.
I don't know. Who can tell?

403
00:19:35,911 --> 00:19:38,447
I'm just... I'm done.
So just go on.

404
00:19:38,465 --> 00:19:40,983
Go back to Japan
and file your report

405
00:19:41,017 --> 00:19:44,955
and tell 'em all
what crappy ambassadors we are.

406
00:19:44,989 --> 00:19:47,825
I can fix car?

407
00:19:47,860 --> 00:19:49,927
What?

408
00:19:49,962 --> 00:19:53,532
Yes. I can fix car.

409
00:19:53,566 --> 00:19:56,034
You can fix car?

410
00:19:56,069 --> 00:19:58,905
Yes. I can fix car.

411
00:19:58,939 --> 00:20:02,542
He can fix car!

412
00:20:02,576 --> 00:20:05,045
I know you're not much
of a hugger, Takayuki,

413
00:20:05,080 --> 00:20:07,615
but you're gettin' one anyway!

414
00:20:07,649 --> 00:20:09,651
Hoo-hoo-hoo!

415
00:20:09,669 --> 00:20:12,170
<i>We never got to Brown county,
but the rest</i>

416
00:20:12,205 --> 00:20:14,156
<i>of Takayuki's visit
just flew by.</i>

417
00:20:14,174 --> 00:20:16,125
<i>Turns out,
he liked to fix stuff.</i>

418
00:20:16,159 --> 00:20:18,511
<i>Fixed our lawn mower,
the toaster,</i>

419
00:20:18,545 --> 00:20:20,998
<i>put batteries
in the smoke detectors,</i>

420
00:20:21,032 --> 00:20:24,301
<i>and it turns out we did have
picture-in-picture on the TV.</i>

421
00:20:24,335 --> 00:20:26,438
Ooh.

422
00:20:26,472 --> 00:20:30,342
<i>In the end,
I think he had a good time.</i>

423
00:20:30,377 --> 00:20:33,178
<i>I think.</i>

424
00:20:33,196 --> 00:20:34,613
<i>I don't know.</i>

425
00:20:34,648 --> 00:20:37,083
<i>The point is,
he enriched our lives.</i>

426
00:20:37,118 --> 00:20:39,886
<i>and I'd like to think
we enriched his.</i>

427
00:20:42,057 --> 00:20:43,024
<i>Zubon wo hake.</i>

428
00:20:44,393 --> 00:20:48,664
<i>Wakatteru yo.
Urusaina.</i>

429
00:20:48,698 --> 00:20:50,466
<i>Urusaina.</i>

430
00:20:51,242 --> 00:20:59,692
Sync by mczolly
Corrected by spyderspyder
www.addic7ed.com

